    {
        "sha": "0d99d1c88a985e3ea9c189942dc2327924b25ddf",
        "node_id": "C_kwDOQ8XTDdoAKDBkOTlkMWM4OGE5ODVlM2VhOWMxODk5NDJkYzIzMjc5MjRiMjVkZGY",
        "commit": {
            "author": {
                "name": "Linus Torvalds",
                "email": "torvalds@linux-foundation.org",
                "date": "2026-01-19T00:04:37Z"
            },
            "committer": {
                "name": "Linus Torvalds",
                "email": "torvalds@linux-foundation.org",
                "date": "2026-01-19T00:04:37Z"
            },
            "message": "Update README about colorization..\n\nI fixed the most obvious interaction with the ANSI color sequences that\ncome from git colorizing its output, and this actually works\nsurprisingly well.  I've been using this _while_ looking at doing the\nhunspell thing in uemacs, and I don't actually hate it.\n\nYes, it ends up bold-facing various random variable names and other\nthings that aren't proper words, but it's less distracting than I\nthought it would be.\n\nI'll still continue to look at uemacs - because catching this while\nwriting commit messages is better than having \"git log\" show them later,\nbut I think I might actually end up using both.\n\nI continue to be surprised that google didn't show any pre-existing\nversion of this silly tool.  The default output from 'hunspell' (and\n'aspell' and friends - I don't want to claim that hunspell would be\nparticularly bad, quite the reverse) has always struck me as fairly\npointless.\n\nSigned-off-by: Linus Torvalds <torvalds@linux-foundation.org>",
